Traditional medicine
{{Expand section|date=November 2025|small=no}}
Despite its reputation as a weed, this plant has been used in folk medicine for a variety of ailments. A tea made from the leaves was taken as a laxative and strong diuretic as well as for jaundice, dropsy, and enteritis with drowsiness.{{citation needed|date=December 2014}} For skin diseases and piles, either a leaf tea or an ointment made from the flowers was used.{{citation needed|date=December 2014}} In addition, a tea made in milk instead of water has been used as an insecticide. Some evidence may support its diuretic and fever-reducing properties.
